1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,396
Welcome to lectures five and six.

2
00:00:02,396 --> 00:00:05,083
Everything becomes a grid of numbers.

3
00:00:05,083 --> 00:00:14,088
We look at images as matrices, audio as waveforms, the curse of dimensionality, and the bias and ethics that come with data.

4
00:00:14,888 --> 00:00:18,307
Three very different things share one representation.

5
00:00:18,307 --> 00:00:21,597
An image is a height by width grid of pixel values:

6
00:00:21,597 --> 00:00:26,693
a twenty eight by twenty eight grey photo is seven hundred eighty four numbers.

7
00:00:26,693 --> 00:00:29,854
A sound is one long list of amplitudes over time:

8
00:00:29,854 --> 00:00:34,563
one second at forty four thousand one hundred hertz is that many numbers.

9
00:00:34,563 --> 00:00:37,208
A document is counts across a vocabulary:

10
00:00:37,208 --> 00:00:40,562
twenty thousand words means twenty thousand numbers.

11
00:00:40,562 --> 00:00:49,400
Each one is a row of a table with hundreds or thousands of columns, and that fact creates both the power and the problem of this lecture.

12
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:53,350
An image is a matrix of brightness values.

13
00:00:53,350 --> 00:00:58,675
Zero is black, two fifty five is white, and everything between is grey.

14
00:00:58,675 --> 00:01:02,875
The shape is height by width and nothing else is stored:

15
00:01:02,875 --> 00:01:05,350
no edges, no objects, no meaning.

16
00:01:05,350 --> 00:01:09,025
Colour adds a third axis for red, green and blue.

17
00:01:09,025 --> 00:01:14,575
A one thousand by one thousand colour photo is over three million numbers:

18
00:01:14,575 --> 00:01:17,200
one row with three million columns.

19
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:21,184
What does E D A look like on a folder of images?

20
00:01:21,184 --> 00:01:23,970
The intensity histogram is the first plot:

21
00:01:23,970 --> 00:01:28,946
a spike at zero or two fifty five means clipped, blown-out or blank images.

22
00:01:28,946 --> 00:01:42,744
Also check that all images have the same size and orientation, that classes are balanced, that there are no duplicates across train and test, and that there are no corrupt files or mixed greyscale and colour.

23
00:01:43,544 --> 00:01:46,681
Audio is a list of numbers sampled very fast.

24
00:01:46,681 --> 00:01:50,445
The sampling rate is how many measurements per second:

25
00:01:50,445 --> 00:01:56,090
forty four thousand one hundred hertz for C D audio, sixteen thousand for speech.

26
00:01:56,090 --> 00:02:02,991
Bit depth is how finely each measurement is recorded; sixteen bit gives sixty five thousand levels.

27
00:02:02,991 --> 00:02:03,618
Channels:

28
00:02:03,618 --> 00:02:05,849
mono is one list, stereo is two.

29
00:02:05,849 --> 00:02:16,304
Ten seconds of speech is a hundred sixty thousand numbers, and nothing about pitch, words or speaker is stored; every one of those has to be computed.

30
00:02:17,104 --> 00:02:19,334
Now a key machine learning idea:

31
00:02:19,334 --> 00:02:21,286
the curse of dimensionality.

32
00:02:21,286 --> 00:02:25,816
Why adding more features can make your data harder to learn from.

33
00:02:26,616 --> 00:02:32,634
In data, a dimension is simply a feature, one column of information about each item:

34
00:02:32,634 --> 00:02:34,640
height, weight, age, income.

35
00:02:34,640 --> 00:02:41,232
A dataset with more columns has higher dimensionality, and that is where the trouble starts.

36
00:02:42,032 --> 00:02:47,914
To keep ten points per side, the space you must fill grows exponentially.

37
00:02:47,914 --> 00:02:50,412
One dimension needs ten points.

38
00:02:50,412 --> 00:02:52,830
Two dimensions need a hundred.

39
00:02:52,830 --> 00:02:55,489
Three dimensions need a thousand.

40
00:02:55,489 --> 00:02:58,712
It is an explosion, not a straight line.

41
00:02:59,512 --> 00:03:02,747
The amount of data almost never grows to match.

42
00:03:02,747 --> 00:03:07,910
The same thousand points that densely filled a line now barely dust a cube.

43
00:03:07,910 --> 00:03:13,072
Models struggle because most of the space around any point is simply empty.

44
00:03:13,872 --> 00:03:18,600
Many algorithms, like K nearest neighbours, rely on near versus far.

45
00:03:18,600 --> 00:03:21,450
In very high dimensions that breaks down.

46
00:03:21,450 --> 00:03:26,109
The ratio of nearest to farthest neighbour distance approaches one.

47
00:03:26,109 --> 00:03:34,104
Every point ends up looking almost equally close to every other point, so nearest neighbour stops being meaningful.

48
00:03:34,904 --> 00:03:36,842
Think of finding a friend.

49
00:03:36,842 --> 00:03:40,347
On a street, one dimension, you find them fast.

50
00:03:40,347 --> 00:03:43,552
In a park, two dimensions, it takes longer.

51
00:03:43,552 --> 00:03:49,517
In a building with floors, three dimensions, you check rooms across many levels.

52
00:03:49,517 --> 00:03:58,016
At a hundred dimensions, which is normal in machine learning, the search space becomes almost impossible to cover.

53
00:03:58,816 --> 00:04:00,945
Four ways to fight the curse.

54
00:04:00,945 --> 00:04:02,267
Feature selection:

55
00:04:02,267 --> 00:04:06,084
keep only the columns that help predict the outcome.

56
00:04:06,084 --> 00:04:07,920
Dimensionality reduction:

57
00:04:07,920 --> 00:04:13,206
techniques like P C A compress many features into a few meaningful ones.

58
00:04:13,206 --> 00:04:14,307
Regularisation:

59
00:04:14,307 --> 00:04:18,271
penalise complexity so the model does not chase noise.

60
00:04:18,271 --> 00:04:23,704
And more relevant data, which helps but rarely outruns exponential growth.

61
00:04:24,504 --> 00:04:27,078
More features is not always better.

62
00:04:27,078 --> 00:04:35,313
Every added dimension expands the space exponentially, spreads data thinner, and makes distance less meaningful.

63
00:04:35,313 --> 00:04:38,328
Choose fewer, more meaningful dimensions.

64
00:04:39,128 --> 00:04:42,719
Suppose you want ten measurements along every axis.

65
00:04:42,719 --> 00:04:46,591
Two features need a hundred points, a comfortable grid.

66
00:04:46,591 --> 00:04:51,450
Six features need a million points, and six is a small dataset today.

67
00:04:51,450 --> 00:04:59,476
A twenty eight by twenty eight image has seven hundred eighty four features, and no dataset ever fills that space.

68
00:04:59,476 --> 00:05:04,616
Adding a column always adds information, and it always empties the space.

69
00:05:05,416 --> 00:05:08,661
Four things go wrong when the space is empty.

70
00:05:08,661 --> 00:05:11,113
Everything is far from everything.

71
00:05:11,113 --> 00:05:17,819
The data hugs the edges, because most of a high-dimensional box's volume is near its surface.

72
00:05:17,819 --> 00:05:25,102
Models overfit easily, because with more columns than rows they can fit any pattern, including noise.

73
00:05:25,102 --> 00:05:29,645
And visualisation stops working beyond two or three dimensions.

74
00:05:29,645 --> 00:05:30,799
First responses:

75
00:05:30,799 --> 00:05:37,000
drop empty columns, build a few meaningful features, and reduce dimensions with P C A.

76
00:05:37,800 --> 00:05:40,548
Bias enters long before the model does.

77
00:05:40,548 --> 00:05:44,775
The world is already unequal and data records it faithfully.

78
00:05:44,775 --> 00:05:49,213
Collection decides who was sampled and who was never reachable.

79
00:05:49,213 --> 00:05:52,243
Labelling adds human judgement and fatigue.

80
00:05:52,243 --> 00:05:55,695
Modelling lets the majority group drive the loss.

81
00:05:55,695 --> 00:06:00,274
And deployment changes behaviour, which changes the next dataset.

82
00:06:00,274 --> 00:06:02,881
Stage five feeds back into stage two:

83
00:06:02,881 --> 00:06:08,376
a system that stops lending to a neighbourhood stops collecting data about it.

84
00:06:09,176 --> 00:06:13,355
Four questions to ask of any dataset you did not collect.

85
00:06:13,355 --> 00:06:13,942
Consent:

86
00:06:13,942 --> 00:06:17,095
did people agree to this use of their data?

87
00:06:17,095 --> 00:06:17,682
Privacy:

88
00:06:17,682 --> 00:06:21,788
can an individual be re-identified by combining columns?

89
00:06:21,788 --> 00:06:22,448
Fairness:

90
00:06:22,448 --> 00:06:28,827
does the error rate differ across groups, since a single accuracy number can hide this?

91
00:06:28,827 --> 00:06:29,707
Consequence:

92
00:06:29,707 --> 00:06:33,739
who is affected by a wrong answer, and can they appeal?

93
00:06:33,739 --> 00:06:38,432
Publicly available answers a legal question, not an ethical one.

94
00:06:39,232 --> 00:06:41,534
What this means for your E D A.

95
00:06:41,534 --> 00:06:44,356
Count rows per group before you model.

96
00:06:44,356 --> 00:06:46,510
Split every summary by group.

97
00:06:46,510 --> 00:06:52,303
Check missingness by group, because dropping rows can remove a group entirely.

98
00:06:52,303 --> 00:06:54,308
Ask where labels came from.

99
00:06:54,308 --> 00:06:56,090
Write a short datasheet:

100
00:06:56,090 --> 00:06:59,803
source, collection method, known gaps, exclusions.

101
00:06:59,803 --> 00:07:02,477
And say what the data cannot answer:

102
00:07:02,477 --> 00:07:08,344
the most useful sentence in many reports begins, this dataset does not tell us.

103
00:07:09,144 --> 00:07:11,500
Numbers in, responsibility out.

104
00:07:11,500 --> 00:07:14,541
An image is a grid of brightness values.

105
00:07:14,541 --> 00:07:21,382
Audio is one long list of amplitudes, and becomes an image once you compute a spectrogram.

106
00:07:21,382 --> 00:07:25,183
Every extra feature multiplies the space to cover.

107
00:07:25,183 --> 00:07:28,223
Bias enters at collection and labelling.

108
00:07:28,223 --> 00:07:33,696
Split by group, count the rows, and write down what the data cannot say.

